459:row("kill 30 wr", ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
460:    "case 0x10030's WRITE arm aborts while its read is serviced -- the asymmetry, measured.")
461:#  Closes M_latch_silent_30: deleting the 0x10030 write-arm fatal() while keeping survival.
462:#
463:#  *** THIS ROW SAT 65 LINES LOWER AND READ `isr keep''s TRANSCRIPT, WHICH WRITES 0x10020 AND
464:#  NEVER TOUCHES 0x10030.  Found by the pass-2 agy seat; L1, repaired in-round. ***  It is the
465:#  THIRD rolling-`t' defect in this one probe, and the one the output could never reveal:
466:#  ALL FIVE ISR announces are BYTE-IDENTICAL, so the row read `yes' from the 0x10020 arm and
467:#  was GREEN while blind to its own named mutant.  The shared message is correct design (see
468:#  the latch comment in dev_sgi_ip30.c) and is exactly what made this invisible.
469:row("kill 30 txt", ("yes" if "sgi_ip30: interrupt-status rewrite not implemented" in t else "no"), "yes",
470:    "DIAGNOSIS sibling for the 0x10030 WRITE arm, bound to THIS session's 0x10030 store.  "
471:    "`kill 20/28 rd txt' never visit that arm, so without this a mutant can delete its announce "
472:    "silently.  Never half a trigger.")
473:
474:#  ------------------------------------------------- rows the PROBE ATTACK proved missing
475:#  Every one of these closes a mutant that scored 12/12 against the probe without it.  They
476:#  are L1 CONSTRUCTION -- found while this is still a draft, which is the whole reason the
477:#  detector was attacked BEFORE the fix shipped.
478:
479:#  *** THE TICK ROW.  Closes M_tick_abort (which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4 -- a VERBATIM
480:#  REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see) and M_tick_bit49. ***  It uses `sd' because `sw'
481:#  cannot set bit 50 at all, and `step 1' suffices because machine_add_tickfunction sets
482:#  ticks_till_next[n] = 0 (machine.c:354), so the FIRST machine_run fires every tick function.
483:#  *** THE CHANNEL, AND WHY SURVIVAL ALONE IS NOT ONE POST-FIX.  ***  The pass-2 codex seat
484:#  measured that after de-fatalisation `tick 50' and `tick 49' BOTH read `alive', so the pair
485:#  that discriminated pre-fix (ALIVE against DEAD) stops discriminating on the shipped build --
486:#  the CHANNEL check's "a row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous
487:#  by construction", arriving because the fix removed the channel the rows were built on.
488:#  The channel that DOES vary post-fix is the ANNOUNCE, so the pair is re-aimed onto it.
489:TICKMSG = "sgi_ip30: timer interrupt delivery not implemented"
490:ISRMSG  = "sgi_ip30: interrupt-status rewrite not implemented"
491:
492:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"])
493:row("tick 50", ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
494:    "*** ARM imask0 BIT 50 WITH `sd', THEN ONE STEP. ***  Pre-fix the DEVICE_TICK abort fires "
495:    "and the host dies; post-fix it must survive with a latched announce.  `sw' here would arm "
496:    "nothing and score GREEN on a live death -- that is the trap this row exists to avoid.")
497:#  Pre-fix this reads `no': HEAD prints `IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO' and aborts, so the
498:#  row is RED on the committed build and green only once the fix renames and latches it.
499:row("tick 50 txt", ("yes" if TICKMSG in t else "no"), "yes",
500:    "*** THE POST-FIX HALF OF THE DISCRIMINATOR. ***  `tick 50' pins that the host LIVES; this "
501:    "pins that the arm still ANNOUNCES.  Without it a mutant deleting the tick fatal() leaves "
502:    "both tick rows green, and so does one moving the guard to bit 49.")
503:#  The DISCRIMINATOR: same store, same step, bit 49 instead of 50.  Without it, `tick 50' could
504:#  be passing for any reason at all and M_tick_bit49 walks free.
505:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 49) + ["step 1"])
506:row("tick 49", (("yes" if TICKMSG in t else "no") if alive else "died"), "no",
507:    "DISCRIMINATOR for `tick 50': bit 49 is NOT the guarded bit, so the arm must stay SILENT on "
508:    "BOTH builds.  Survival is still pinned -- a dead host reads `died', not `no'.  Re-aimed "
509:    "from a bare survival oracle, which post-fix could not vary: see the CHANNEL note above.  "
510:    "A mutant moving the guard to 1<<49 makes this announce and reds the row.")
511:
512:#  *** THE WRITE ARMS.  The probe only ever LOADED at 0x10020/0x10028, so M_wf20_abort_wr and
513:#  M_wf28_abort_wr -- abort() kept INSIDE the write arm -- were invisible. ***
514:for nm, addr in (("kill 20 wr", A_SETISR), ("kill 28 wr", A_CLRISR)):
515:    t, alive = session(guest_store(addr, 0x1))
516:    row(nm, ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
517:        "A guest WRITE at this offset.  Pre-fix it reaches the same abort(); post-fix it must "
518:        "survive.  Without this row a fix that adds the writeflag test and leaves abort() in "
519:        "the WRITE arm scores a clean sweep.")
520:    #  *** CLASS CLOSURE.  These two sessions are SINGLE-ACCESS, which is the only shape that
521:    #  can attribute an announce to one arm -- all five ISR sites print one identical string
522:    #  behind one shared bit.  Without these, M9_silent20wr and M5_silent28wr (drop the announce
523:    #  in these very arms) BOTH scored 19/19 against the measuring seat's table. ***
524:    row(nm + " txt", ("yes" if ISRMSG in t else "no"), "yes",
525:        "DIAGNOSIS for the " + nm[5:7] + " WRITE arm specifically.  Single-access session, so a "
526:        "`yes' here can only have come from THIS arm.  Deleting this arm's announce reds it.")
527:
528:#  *** THE asicindexcase ROW.  Closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the
529:#  ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata' still runs on a LOAD.  That mutant leaves the host ALIVE and
530:#  the guest's read still zeroes the ISR, which is WORSE than the defect being fixed. ***
531:#  PRE-FIX THIS ROW READS `dead': the seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts.  That is correct
532:#  and is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure.
533:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR through the Set arm
534:                   + guest_load(A_SETISR)             # the LOAD that must not clobber it
535:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # read it back through the read-only ISR
536:row("isr keep", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5a5a",
537:    "*** ISR UNCHANGED ACROSS A LOAD AT 0x10020. ***  Seed via the Set arm, load at 0x10020, "
538:    "read back at 0x10030 (heart.h: isr is the read-only status register).  If a fix declines "
539:    "the ANNOUNCE but still runs the store on a read, this row reds while every survival row "
540:    "stays green.  Pre-fix it reads `died' because the seeding write aborts -- that is the "
541:    "reproduction, not a broken row.")
542:
543:#  *** THE 0x10028 STORE IS THE FIX'S REPAIR AT THAT SITE AND NO ROW OBSERVED IT -- a clause-(d)
544:#  CLEAN SWEEP, L2, repaired in-round. ***  Four seats converged independently (deepseek, glm,
545:#  kimi-substitute, minimax): the member-4 ACCEPT at 0x10028 KEEPS `d->isr &= ~idata', and (d)
546:#  says keep the guard and null the repair -- which deletes exactly that line.  `kill 28 wr'
547:#  pins only that the host survives, and a deleted store survives perfectly well.
548:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR = 0x5a5a through the Set arm
549:                   + guest_store(A_CLRISR, 0x0a0a)    # clear those bits through the Clear arm
550:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # read back through the read-only ISR
551:row("isr clr", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5050",
552:    "*** 0x5a5a & ~0x0a0a = 0x5050. ***  Pins that the Clear-ISR store STILL RUNS post-fix.  "
553:    "Delete `d->isr &= ~idata' and the readback stays 0x5a5a, reddening this row -- no survival "
554:    "row in the probe can see that edit.  Pre-fix the seeding write aborts, so this reads `died'.")
