556:#  *** THE DROPPED CLEAR-ON-WRITE AT 0x10030 -- named by agy, glm and the kimi substitute, and
557:#  the ONLY row that pins it stays dropped. ***  heart.h marks `isr' read-only, so this round
558:#  DELETED the clear.  Re-inserting it is a (b)-adjacent mutant that every survival row passes.
559:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR = 0x5a5a
560:                   + guest_store(A_ISR, 0x1234)       # write the READ-ONLY register
561:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # it must come back UNTOUCHED
562:row("isr drop 30", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5a5a",
563:    "*** THE WRITE MUST BE DISCARDED. ***  Re-inserting `d->isr &= ~idata' here gives "
564:    "0x5a5a & ~0x1234 = 0x484a and reds this row.  `kill 30 wr' pins survival only and is "
565:    "structurally blind to it -- the refusal-EFFECT channel, not the refusal.")
566:
567:#  =====================================================================================
568:#  THE LATCH-MECHANISM CELLS.  Every row above asks WHETHER an arm announced; these ask
569:#  HOW MANY TIMES and FROM WHICH BIT.  The measuring seat built two one-character mutants
570:#  that no presence oracle can see, and both scored 19/19.
571:#  =====================================================================================
572:
573:#  *** CROSS-BIT.  Closes the bit-collision mutant (IP30_W_ISR (1<<1) -> (1<<0)). ***
574:#  Recipe MEASURED by the opus5 seat, and its first attempt was a DEFECTIVE APPARATUS worth
575:#  recording: machine_add_tickfunction sets ticks_till_next[n] = 0, so the tick's one free
576:#  firing lands on the session's FIRST machine_run -- a seeding store BEFORE the arm consumes
577:#  it, and the row then says nothing.  ARM BIT 50 FIRST.
578:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"] + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1))
579:row("xbit isr", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
580:    "*** TICK FIRST, THEN AN ISR ACCESS, IN ONE DEVICE LIFETIME. ***  Both classes must "
581:    "announce, from their OWN bits.  Collapse IP30_W_ISR onto IP30_W_TICK and the tick consumes "
582:    "the bit first, so the ISR arm falls silent and this reads 0.  No fresh-process row can see "
583:    "that mutant at all -- it needs both announce classes in ONE lifetime.")
584:row("xbit tick", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
585:    "The other half of the same session: the TICK announce is still exactly one.  Together with "
586:    "`xbit isr' this pins that the two bits are INDEPENDENT, which is the claim the round makes "
587:    "in product code and which nothing measured before now.")
588:
589:#  *** ANNOUNCE COUNT.  Closes the latch-defeat mutant ((1<<1) -> (0<<1)). ***
590:#  A presence oracle reads `yes' whether the latch works or not; only a COUNT varies.
591:#  *** RE-AIMED 2026-09-18 ONTO THE WRITE ARMS, BECAUSE THE F13 SILENT-DECLINE REPAIR DESTROYED
592:#  THE CHANNEL THIS ROW WAS BUILT ON.  Measured: it read got=0 want=1 the moment the read arms
593:#  went silent -- vacuous by construction, exactly as its own CHANNEL note warns. ***
594:#
595:#  THAT IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS ROUND A FIX HAS REMOVED THE CHANNEL A ROW RESTED ON: the tick
596:#  pair when de-fatalisation removed the DEATH channel, `kill 20/28 rd txt' when the silent
597:#  decline removed the ANNOUNCE, and now this.  The general form is worth more than the fix:
598:#  *a repair does not only have to be correct, it has to be checked against every row whose
599:#  oracle reads the channel it changes* -- and a row so invalidated goes GREEN or RED for a
600:#  reason unrelated to the mutant it names.
601:#
602:#  Two WRITE accesses still announce (0x10020-WR and 0x10028-WR are member-4 ACCEPT arms that
603:#  keep their store AND announce), so the latch-defeat channel survives there.
604:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1) + guest_store(A_CLRISR, 0x1))
605:row("latch 1x", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
606:    "TWO declined ISR accesses, ONE announce -- that is what the unqualified `(once)' marker "
607:    "in the message text CLAIMS, so this row is what makes the marker true rather than decorative.  "
608:    "Defeat the latch and it reads 2.")
609:
610:#  *** THE DECLINED READ'S VALUE.  Closes the mirror mutant grok named at design time. ***
611:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a) + guest_load(A_SETISR))
612:row("isr 20 rd val", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x0",
613:    "A declined READ at 0x10020 returns ZERO, not a mirror of d->isr.  `odata = d->isr' here "
614:    "would read 0x5a5a and red this row.  The ISR is seeded NON-ZERO on purpose: against a "
615:    "zero ISR the mutant and the fix are indistinguishable, which is how a value row goes "
616:    "vacuous without looking it.")
617:
618:#  *** SIBLING PARITY -- THE SYMMETRIC CELL, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS A FLOOR BREACH IN THE CLASS
619:#  CLOSURE ITSELF.  Required by the agy seat; grok independently named it and diagnosed the
620:#  cause. ***  The two READ arms are structurally identical -- both decline, both announce,
621:#  both leave odata 0 -- so closing the declined-VALUE cell at 0x10020 and not at 0x10028 is
622:#  the #499 shape exactly: repaired at the address the mutant used, symmetric cell left open.
623:#
624:#  THE CAUSE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE ROW, and it was in the round's own pre-table block:
625:#  PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as "decline, no value to pin".  *** A DECLINE HAS A
626:#  VALUE, AND IT IS 0. ***  That framing is what made the miss mechanical -- the enumeration
627:#  asked which arms ANNOUNCE and treated a refusal as having no observable, when the refusal's
628:#  own returned value is exactly the channel M10 travels on.
629:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a) + guest_load(A_CLRISR))
630:row("isr 28 rd val", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x0",
631:    "A declined READ at 0x10028 returns ZERO, not a mirror of d->isr -- the sibling of "
632:    "`isr 20 rd val'.  Seed via 0x10020 so the ISR is NON-ZERO: against a zero ISR the mirror "
633:    "mutant and the fix are indistinguishable, which is how a value row goes vacuous without "
634:    "looking it.  `kill 28 rd txt' stays GREEN under that mutant (the announce still fires), "
635:    "so no other row in this probe can see it.")
636:
637:#  =====================================================================================
638:#  THE LATCH-AXIS CLASS CLOSURE.  Obligation 5 of the fable5 adjudication, 2026-09-18.
639:#
640:#  *** THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE WITH TWO FAILURE MODES.  It is one
641:#  `d->warned |= IP30_W_*' beside EVERY announce -- the same duplication the ANNOUNCE axis was
642:#  decomposed for, and leaving it atomic is how six mutants swept a 27-row instrument. ***
643:#
644:#  THE SPACE, enumerated from the function's own inputs:
645:#      {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets}  +  {2 collision directions} = TEN.
646:#  It was FOURTEEN before the F13 silent-decline repair removed the two READ-arm announces --
647:#  resolving that design question REMOVED four cells of detector work rather than adding them.
648:#
649:#  ALREADY CLOSED (6): the four announce rows (kill 20 wr txt, kill 28 wr txt, kill 30 txt,
650:#  tick 50 txt), the 0x10020-WR latch-set (latch 1x), and the TICK->ISR collision (xbit).
651:#  CLOSED HERE (4): the remaining three latch-set cells and the reverse collision direction.
652:#
653:#  *** THE SECOND TICK IS FORCEABLE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. ***  The
654:#  adjudicator allowed an UNBUILDABLE cell to be disclosed with its apparatus reason, but only
655:#  after being measured unbuildable.  MEASURED, and the artifact is named because a cited
656:#  measurement must live at a durable path -- regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/
657:#  tickcount_20260918.out, sentinel TICKCOUNT_DONE, REPLICATED on an independent re-run.
658:#  Counting ticks by the unconditional `reg_0x20000 += 1000':
659:#        step 1 / 2 / 3 / 8 / 1000 -> ONE tick      step 65536 / 70000 -> TWO
660:#        step 140000 -> THREE
661:#  *** STOPPING AT `step 8' WOULD HAVE DISCLOSED TWO BUILDABLE CELLS AS UNBUILDABLE. ***  The
662:#  threshold is ~65536 because machine.c decrements ticks_till_next by ONE per single-step.
663:#  So these rows are SLOW by construction, not by accident; that is the price of the cell.
664:#  =====================================================================================
665:
666:#  Closes MB/MF/MG-shaped mutants at the two remaining ISR write arms: delete that arm's
667:#  `d->warned |= IP30_W_ISR' and it announces on EVERY access instead of once.
668:for _nm, _a in (("latch 28wr", A_CLRISR), ("latch 30wr", A_ISR)):
669:    t, alive = session(guest_store(_a, 0x1) + guest_store(_a, 0x1))
670:    row(_nm, (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
671:        "TWO accesses at THIS ARM ALONE, so the count attributes to it.  `latch 1x' cannot: it "
672:        "visits 0x10020 then 0x10028 and the FIRST access latches, leaving the second arm's "
673:        "latch-set never load-bearing -- which is why MF_nolatchset28rd escaped 27/27.")
674:
675:#  Closes ME_nolatchsettick -- the worst of the eight: one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50
676:#  would otherwise give AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK, FOREVER.  Guest-drivable floodclass.
677:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 65536"])
678:row("latch tick", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
679:    "*** TWO TICKS IN ONE LIFETIME (step 65536 -- MEASURED to fire exactly two). ***  Delete the "
680:    "tick's `d->warned |= IP30_W_TICK' and this reads 2, then 3, then forever.  No single-tick "
681:    "row can see it: with one tick the latched and unlatched builds are identical.")
682:
683:#  Closes MA_assign20wr -- the ISR->TICK collision direction, which `xbit' runs BACKWARDS.
684:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"]
685:                   + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1) + ["step 65536"])
686:row("xbit rev", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
687:    "*** TICK -> ISR -> TICK: the order the product comment's own hazard needs, and the "
688:    "OPPOSITE of `xbit'. ***  Under `|=' -> `=' at the ISR arm the assignment CLOBBERS the tick "
689:    "bit, so the second tick announces again and this reads 2.  `xbit' runs tick->ISR and stops, "
690:    "so it cannot see the clobber -- which is why MA, a ONE-CHARACTER edit, scored 27/27.")
691:
692:#  *** THE MIRROR OF `xbit rev', AND ITS ABSENCE WAS MEASURED: V2_assigntick SCORED 32/32
693:#  AGAINST A CLOSURE THAT CLAIMED BOTH COLLISION DIRECTIONS. ***
694:#
695:#  A CLOBBER CAN ONLY BITE IF THE CLOBBERED CLASS'S BIT WAS ALREADY SET.  `xbit isr' runs
696:#  TICK -> ISR, so when the tick assigns, the ISR bit has never been set, nothing is lost, and
697:#  the row is green on the mutant.  Seeing it needs ISR FIRST:
698:#
699:#      ISR announces -> warned = IP30_W_ISR
700:#      tick ASSIGNS  -> warned = IP30_W_TICK   <- the ISR bit is LOST
701:#      ISR again     -> the guard passes again -> TWO announces where one is correct
702:#
703:#  `xbit rev' is the same idea with the operands swapped (ISR clobbers TICK).  Building one
704:#  direction and not its mirror is the #499 shape -- repaired at the address the mutant used,
705:#  symmetric cell left open -- and it is the SAME defect the opus5 seat diagnosed in the
706:#  ORIGINAL `xbit', reproduced inside the repair for it.
707:#
708:#  The 65536 steps are load-bearing: the tick must fire BETWEEN the two ISR accesses, and the
709:#  first tick of a session is consumed by the first `run_ops'.  Measured, not assumed.
710:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1)
711:                   + guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 65536"]
712:                   + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1))
713:row("xbit rev2", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
714:    "*** ISR -> TICK -> ISR: the mirror of `xbit rev', and the only order in which a TICK-side "
715:    "assignment can be seen to clobber the ISR bit. ***  Correct build announces ONCE (the "
716:    "latch holds across the tick); make the tick's latch set an ASSIGNMENT and the second ISR "
717:    "access announces again, reading 2.  `xbit isr' runs tick-first and is structurally blind "
718:    "to it -- V2_assigntick scored 32/32 against it.")
719:
720:
721:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ report
722:CONTROL = "OK"
723:for nm, got, want, _ in rows:
724:    if nm in ("live", "devsig") and got != want:
725:        CONTROL = "DEAD"
726:
727:print("ip30_abort_probe -- binary=%s label=%s machine=sgi/ip30" % (BIN, LABEL))
728:print("IP30ABORT_CONTROL=%s" % CONTROL)
729:if CONTROL == "DEAD":
730:    print("IP30ABORT_RESULT=0/0")
731:    print("  *** SETUP FAILED -- the vehicle is not the vehicle. NOT 'no defect'. ***")
732:    for nm, got, want, note in rows:
733:        print("  %-12s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s" % (nm, got, want, note[:60]))
734:    print("IP30ABORT_DONE")
735:    raise SystemExit(0)
736:
737:n = 0
738:for nm, got, want, note in rows:
739:    ok = (got == want)
740:    n += 1 if ok else 0
741:    print("  %-12s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s  %s" % (nm, got, want, "ok" if ok else "FAIL", note[:70]))
742:print("IP30ABORT_RESULT=%d/%d" % (n, len(rows)))
743:print("IP30ABORT_DONE")

