tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50280:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/EXITHUNT_grok_20260917.txt; src/devices/dev_mvme187.c:63-68,127-129; src/include/thirdparty/mvme_memcreg.h:31-; src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:88",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50314:   "state_note": "`dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm does `fatal(\"[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]\")` and then `cpu->running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- so the access 'succeeds' while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu->running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat's claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the `default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  **NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY 'the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS' CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu->running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -> `bus_pci_add(..., \"ali_m1543\")` -> `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50322:     "ev": "src/devices/dev_8259.c:140,231,235; regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/EXITHUNT_grok_20260917.txt",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50323:     "note": "`dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm does `fatal(\"[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]\")` and then `cpu->running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- so the access 'succeeds' while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu->running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat's claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the `default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  **NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. ***"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50344:   "state_note": "`dev_lunafb.c` halts the CPU on ordinary framebuffer offsets: the `lunafb_a` and `lunafb_b` `default:` arms and a ROP-not-all-ones write all do `cpu->running = 0`.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:93`, `:160`, `:166`, `:185`. *** THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN QUEUED -- `lunafb` had ZERO mentions in the entire ledger before this row. ***  It is installed unconditionally from `DEVINIT(luna88k)`, and luna88k is a machine this project HAS a rig for and DOES boot, which is what separates it from the ip30 case (advertised but no in-tree boot image). REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED**: the seat's claim is that `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` and `BMAP_BMSEL`, so every other offset in that window stops the CPU, and that a comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH is wrong while the `#define`s are right.  It also claims OpenBSD's `omfb_clear_framebuffer` uses BMSEL, which IS implemented, so the boot path is BELIEVED clear of the halt.  **None of that is re-verified here.**  FAULT (member 5) is architecturally available on M88K and `return 0` would deliver it -- today the halt runs first. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW'S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on 'a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS', implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit->machine->x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt's 'CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k' is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD's `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`'s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50352:     "ev": "src/devices/dev_lunafb.c:93,160,166,185; regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/EXITHUNT_grok_20260917.txt",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50353:     "note": "`dev_lunafb.c` halts the CPU on ordinary framebuffer offsets: the `lunafb_a` and `lunafb_b` `default:` arms and a ROP-not-all-ones write all do `cpu->running = 0`.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:93`, `:160`, `:166`, `:185`. *** THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN QUEUED -- `lunafb` had ZERO mentions in the entire ledger before this row. ***  It is installed unconditionally from `DEVINIT(luna88k)`, and luna88k is a machine this project HAS a rig for and DOES boot, which is what separates it from the ip30 case (advertised but no in-tree boot image). REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED**: the seat's claim is that `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` and `BMAP_BMSEL`, so every other offset in that window stops the CPU, and that a comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH is wrong while the `#define`s are right.  It also claims OpenBSD's `omfb_clear_framebuffer` uses BMSEL, which IS implemented, so the boot path is BELIEVED clear of the halt.  **None of that is re-verified here.**  FAULT (member 5) is architecturally available on M88K and `return 0` would deliver it -- today the halt runs first. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. ***"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50370:   "witness": "none -- documented-only.  ANSWERED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR on both sides: armmsr's reachability by mode enumeration (7 handled, 25 unhandled, none producible by a correct in-tree kernel), and ip30's by gate_asan_sweep.sh:593 running ip30 green.  The ruling is ip30abortkill first.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50374:   "state_note": "*** BOTH OPEN HIGHs' SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50383:     "note": "*** BOTH OPEN HIGHs' SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50394:     "note": "*** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50519:   "state_note": "*** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT'd, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width's session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT 'has WNOHANG'.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat's own words -- *'a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings'* -- and this project's addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`'s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50528:     "note": "*** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT'd, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width's session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT 'has WNOHANG'.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat's own words -- *'a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings'* -- and this project's addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`'s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it.",
