49854:   "title": "dev_sgi_ip30.c aborts the host on a guest load OR store at 0x0ff10020 on the advertised -E sgi -e ip30, and the read arm zeroes the ISR before dying; a fourth abort() is armed by a LEGAL imask0 write and fires from DEVICE_TICK. An exit( sweep cannot see any of them.",
49855:   "state_note": "*** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  `case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  `case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & (1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and `vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month.  *** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, `IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- which IS the reproduction, since the house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30' with no `device_add' introduced to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd' DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd' alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE REPORTED `alive' FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF instead of waiting out its timeout, so the `spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single `waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB core, reports 'still running', and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample once** -- and `arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its witness will certify the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the same disagreement: my `devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit R10000, so a CORRECT device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 3 + MEMBER 4, AND THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY ARGUMENT. ***  I filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` occurs FIVE times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- with no definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` (`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  **Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the inference that it was cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil literals use a DELETED numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick's `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating them onto `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 16550 breaks on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 7 on `lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP ROW** (implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  *Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *'Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.'*  **The correct statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- `interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The distinction is not pedantic: 'not available' would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4'S WORD 'SEPARATE' IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling it 'separate' is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi's sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the missing test **is the read-kill's entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** -- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d->isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store updates *'a register nothing reads for any purpose'*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`'s READ returns `d->isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive).  The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3's warrant cited a PROM that 'already talks to 0x10018' -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME's *'one bit per site, never shared'* than from LCA's two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write silence the timer-tick diagnostic forever** (grok, minimax, agy).  Disposition 7 holds AS A CENSUS but does NOT pick member 2 over member 3: the `fatal()` already exists, so de-fatalising KEEPS it and latches (grok). **STILL OPEN AND NOT INVENTED:** minimax says Q3 -- what a read at 0x10020 should RETURN post-fix -- **cannot be settled from the packet** and needs an IP30/HEART source, and there is no HEART manual under `_scratchpad/`.  That is an honest UNKNOWN and it outranks a confident guess; the round picks a disposition for Q3 with its reason stated, or defers the read behaviour.  **agy proposes a TICK ROW BY `step 70000`** -- `tickshift` 16 means 65536 cycles -- which would make the tick reachable WITHOUT free-run and **contradicts my 'needs free-run' statement in this row's witness limit.**  Not yet measured; carried as a named question rather than adopted.  *** THE TICK ROW IS BUILDABLE AND IS MEASURED: `step 1' KILLS THE HOST.  The codex/agy split is settled BY MEASUREMENT, and BOTH SEATS WERE HALF RIGHT WHILE THE DECISIVE FACT WAS NAMED BY NEITHER. ***  codex was right that stepping alone is insufficient -- the guard must be ARMED (measured: no store + 70000 steps -> alive; bit 49 + 70000 steps -> alive).  agy was right that free-run is NOT needed, but its stated reason -- a 65536-cycle period implying `step 70000' -- is not why the cheap row works.  **THE MECHANISM, verified by me in the source and not taken from the seat:** `machine_add_tickfunction' sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0' (`machine.c:354') while `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 << tickshift' (`:355'), and `machine_run()' decrements then fires while `<= 0' (`:727').  **So the FIRST `machine_run()' of any session drives it to -1 and fires EVERY tick function immediately** -- `step 1' executes the store and then runs the tick in that same `machine_run()'.  *** A THIRD FACT NEITHER SEAT NAMED, AND IT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: **BIT 50 CANNOT BE SET BY `sw'.** ***  `case 0x10000' does `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a FOUR-BYTE store caps idata below 2^32 -- measured, `sw 0xffffffff' + 70000 steps -> ALIVE.  Arming requires **`sd' = 0xFCE80000** (readback `ld' = 0xDC220000).  **`OP_SW_T0_A3' IS THE ONLY STORE HELPER IN `ip30_abort_probe.py'**, so a tick row copy-pasted from it would arm nothing, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH.  **PERIODICITY IS NOT A STARTUP ARTIFACT** -- the boundary was measured from TWO different warm offsets, both sides: warm=10 gives alive at N=65525 and DEAD at 65526; warm=100 gives alive at 65435 and DEAD at 65436.  Both put tick #2 at `machine_run' #65536 exactly, so ticks fire at MR #1, #65536, #131072.  **A guest arming bit 50 at ANY moment dies within 65536 instructions, with no access in flight.**  *The seat's own first derivation said #65537 and its boundary run measured that false -- it recorded the measurement over its derivation, which is the right way round.*  STABILITY: run 1 and run 2 are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 13 scored rows (9/13, same four FAILs), `IP30TICK_CONTROL=OK` both times.  CONTROLS ALL GREEN, including the two that make the positive attributable: **`tick49 s70k'** (same store, same step count, bit 49 not 50 -> alive) is the discriminator, and **`late s1'** (armed AFTER tick #1 was consumed, one step -> alive) is what proves `tick50 s1' measured THE TICK and not something else about the first instruction.  **L4 APPLIED TO MY OWN PROBE:** `ip30_abort_probe.py`'s `acc imask' row claimed \"a handful of steps cannot reach it\".  MEASURED FALSE.  The row is still safe, for two reasons it did not state (bit 18, and `sw' cannot set bit 50 anyway), and the comment now says so.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND:** codex's constraint is DISCHARGEABLE rather than blocking -- the four-site closure is available because the tick row exists and is measured.  Artifacts at `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/`, all four carrying their completion sentinel, copied off the volatile scratch path the measuring seat itself flagged.  *** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says \"according to Linux/IP30\" three times, so the reference is Linux's `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner's approval on this project's vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d->isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register's store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR IT IN ITS OWN COMMENT: heart.h documents `isr` (0x10030) as READ-ONLY, and the fork implements a WRITE arm -- `/* Clear-on-write (TODO: is this correct?) */  d->isr &= ~idata;`.  The cited document answers that TODO: NO. ***  Clearing goes through `clear_isr` at 0x10028, which this same file already implements, so the model has TWO clear paths where the architecture has ONE, and the fabricated one is the one carrying the self-doubting comment.  IN SCOPE for this round (same case labels, same function, same detector); it is a member 6 BACKED ACCEPT standing WITHOUT a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit.  Disposition deliberately NOT decided here. **HONEST LIMIT, STATED:** a Linux header is a driver author's model, NOT SGI's specification. It is strong evidence about what the registers ARE and about what this emulator MEANT -- because this emulator cites it -- and it is not a hardware contract.  No claim above depends on the difference: declining a read is safe under either reading.  Preserved at regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/HEART_reference_20260917.txt.  *** FABLE 5.1 ADJUDICATION, 2026-09-17.  IDENTITY VERIFIED -- `claude-fable-5-1`, the nonce, AND the verbatim first line of CHECKPOINT.md, so prompt delivery and file access are proven rather than assumed.  Fired LAST, after all 8 scriptable transports and the Opus measurement. *** **SHIP ORDER: (c) commit the reviewed working set -> (b) run `arm_msrmode_probe.py` -> (a) the ip30 fix as its OWN round.**  Serialisation note it supplied and I had not: (b) runs the emulator and (c) runs a gate, and they contend on CPU but NOT on the tree -- so run (b) to completion, THEN (c)'s gate alone. **A2 -- Q3 IS ALREADY ANSWERED AND THE FIX DOES NOT WAIT.**  DECLINE the read at 0x10020; do not mirror `d->isr`, do not return 0-as-a-value.  *** AND IT RULED ON A SECOND DISPOSITION MY FIVE QUESTIONS NEVER REACHED: DECLINE THE 0x10030 **WRITE** ARM TOO. ***  heart.h documents `isr` as read-only and the fork's clear-on-write is a member-6 BACKED ACCEPT standing without a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit -- Linux/IP30 clears via 0x10028, which the file already implements.  Same function, same case labels, same detector family, so same round.  It owes a STATE row (write declined AND `isr` unchanged), not merely an `alive' row. **A3 -- MY FRAMING WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART.**  These are not four defects competing to join a round under the reopening rule.  **They are ONE ABANDONED FEATURE** -- the identical `fatal(\"IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO\"); abort();` at every reachable exit of a rewrite nobody finished.  The unit of work is the FEATURE, not the site, and splitting it would de-fatalise three of four exits and leave the fourth a live host death, which is the de-escalation clause's own hazard.  *** AND IT REFUSED TO LET ME GLOSS THE COST: the 'no new oracle' criterion IS genuinely violated -- the tick needs its own periodicity oracle and its own latch bit, so THE ROUND SHIPS TWO DETECTORS, and the round record must NAME that deviation rather than let a two-probe structure read as an accident. *** **A4 -- IT FOUND MY WEAKEST CORRECTION AND IT IS RIGHT.**  My (iv) said the `acc imask' row is safe *because it writes bit 18 AND because `sw' cannot set bit 50*.  Both clauses are true and **clause (2) MISDIRECTS**: the row's value `1<<18` has no high bits at any width, so width is irrelevant to THIS row -- while 'sw cannot set bit 50' reads as a general safety invariant for imask0, and **the death IS reachable, by `sd 1<<50`**.  *** MY OWN PROBE'S COMMENT IS STRONGER AND MORE HONEST THAN MY SUMMARY OF IT: it calls that row a documented HAZARD and warns that copy-pasting it as a kill row would 'arm NOTHING, stay ALIVE, and score green on a live host death'.  My correction re-labelled a HAZARD as a SAFETY PROPERTY. ***  That is the a-correction-is-not-safer-than-what-it-corrects rule landing on me. **A4(i) -- MY WARRANT OVERCLAIMED TRANSFERABILITY, verified locally:** `dev_sgi_ip32.c` has 6 INTERRUPT_CONNECT/ASSERT sites; **`dev_sgi_ip30.c` has ZERO, and zero `struct interrupt` members.**  'CRIME uses it' proves the IDIOM exists on MIPS; it does not prove member 7 is BUILDABLE for ip30, which has no interrupt topology at all.  The correct statement is *member 7's idiom is available; its application to ip30 requires building the HEART topology and is substantial* -- which is the carrier's own 2026-09-06 lesson (check the member is available on THIS CPU; a precedent real elsewhere is not thereby transferable). **A5 -- THE QUESTION I WAS NOT ASKING, AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE: DOES `ip30abortkill` ACTUALLY OUTRANK `armmsrmodekill`?**  Rung is a FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY orders the queue, and severity rests on real-guest reachability **that is measured for NEITHER**.  Verified locally: `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels today**; only one gate names ip30 at all, and the device's own header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  Meanwhile `armmsrmodekill` kills the PROCESS on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  **So the order between the two HIGHs was set without the measurement that would settle it** -- which is itself the argument for running the arm probe BEFORE the larger ip30 build: the cheap measurement informs the order of the expensive work.  *** THE FIX DIFF IS WRITTEN AND PRESERVED, AND IT FOUND A HOLE IN MY OWN DETECTOR. ***  `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt`.  NOT APPLIED -- the adjudicated ship order puts the ip30 round LAST, after the commit and the arm probe. The diff honours every ruling: TWO latch bits (`IP30_W_TICK` 1<<0, `IP30_W_ISR` 1<<1, 'one bit per site, never shared'), the `(once)` house marker, `break` and never an early `return` so the common tail at :180-183 still runs, the `writeflag` test at 0x10020/0x10028, and on a READ it DECLINES rather than mirroring -- *'odata stays the function's 0 initializer; that is not a modelled value'*.  It also declines the 0x10030 WRITE arm per the Fable ruling, with the comment *'the TODO answers no.  Clearing goes through clear_isr at 0x10028'*.  It carries a control-flow table showing every path lives. *** THE DETECTOR FINDING, AND IT IS FLOOR-RELEVANT: `kill 30 wr` IS SURVIVE-ONLY AND THEREFORE CANNOT SEE THIS REPAIR. ***  Post-fix the host lives on a 0x10030 write whether or not the store is applied, so an `alive` oracle is invariant under the very edit the row exists to pin.  **That row owes a STATE oracle -- write declined AND `isr` unchanged** -- which is the CHANNEL check's vacuous-by-construction shape, knowable now, before any table runs. **AND THE ISR-UNCHANGED ROW MUST COVER BOTH LOADS, with a twist at 0x10028:** `&= ~0` is a no-op today, so a row that only checks the ISR CELL cannot fail there.  Pin **`odata` is not `d->isr`** instead, seeding ISR to a distinctive value first.  Without that, the 0x10028 writeflag repair ships unpinned.  *** THE DETECTOR WAS ATTACKED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED, AND IT HAS CLEAN-SWEEP (d) MUTANTS. Every one is L1 CONSTRUCTION -- pre-ship material still being built -- NOT an L2 breach, because nothing has been committed. ***  That is the whole point of attacking a detector while it is still a draft. **THE MISSES, each 12/12 against the current 12-row probe:** **(1) `M_tick_abort` -- keep the `if`, put `abort()` back.**  MISS.  No session sets imask0 bit 50, so nothing in the probe visits the tick guard at all.  *** THIS IS CLAUSE (b) AT SITE 4 AS WELL AS (d): a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect that the detector cannot see. ***  **(2) `M_tick_bit49`** -- `(1<<50)` -> `(1<<49)`.  MISS: `acc imask` deliberately writes bit 18, and `tick49 s70k` was ALREADY MEASURED alive.  **(3) `M_tick_swap`** -- `imask0` -> `reg_0x20000`, a field the body writes every tick.  MISS: `acc 20000` stores 0x1234 so bit 50 stays clear. **(4) `M_wf20_store_out` -- the `writeflag` wraps only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a LOAD.**  MISS.  **This is the `asicindexcase` (d) shape exactly: the guard is kept and the repair that stops the clobber is nulled** -- the host lives and the guest's load still zeroes the ISR.  *** AND PASS 1 ALREADY ASKED FOR THIS ROW (Q5) AND IT IS STILL NOT THERE -- which is the `#490` lesson repeating: a prescribed row lost between the pass that prescribed it and the list that was written. ***  **(5) `M_wf20_abort_wr` / `M_wf28_abort_wr`** -- `writeflag` present, `abort()` kept INSIDE the write arm.  MISS, because there is no `kill 20 wr` and no `kill 28 wr` row: the probe only ever LOADS at those two offsets. **TWO HONEST NON-BREACHES, and recording them matters as much as the misses:** `M_wf28_store_out` is **EQUIVALENT, not a breach** -- `idata` is 0 on reads (`:87-90`) so `&= ~0` is a no-op, and *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT*.  `M_one_latch_bit` (tick and ISR sharing one `warned`) is **NOT a (d) floor cell**, because the two guards live in DIFFERENT FUNCTIONS and DEPENDS-ON is walked only to the enclosing function boundary, never into callers -- so it is **L3 / contract disclosure, not L2**.  *That bound is what stops this becoming `#392`, and the seat applied it against its own finding.* **ROWS OWED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPS:** a TICK row (arm bit 50 with `sd`, step, expect the announce -- note the existing tick witness is a SEPARATE probe, so either it folds in or the contract must state the round ships TWO detectors); **`kill 20 wr` and `kill 28 wr`**; the **ISR-UNCHANGED** row (seed ISR, load at 0x10020, read back at 0x10030); and a `txt` sibling for the 0x10030 write so `M_latch_silent_30` cannot delete the diagnostic silently. **AND ONE FORWARD-LOOKING MUTANT WORTH THE CONTRACT:** `M_wf20_len8` -- `writeflag == MEM_WRITE && len == 8` -- misses today only because there is no write row at 0x10020, and **HEART registers are `u64`, so it becomes real the moment a write row is added using `sw`**.  That is the `sd`/`sw` trap from the tick measurement arriving in the detector.  *** THE OWED ROWS ARE WRITTEN (L1 CONSTRUCTION, NOT YET RUN). ***  Five mutants that scored 12/12 against the probe are now closed by construction: **`tick 50`** (closes M_tick_abort -- which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see -- and M_tick_swap); **`tick 49`**, its DISCRIMINATOR, so `tick 50` cannot pass for an unrelated reason and M_tick_bit49 cannot walk free; **`kill 20 wr` / `kill 28 wr`**, because the probe only ever LOADED at those offsets so an abort() kept inside the WRITE arm was invisible; **`isr keep`**, the `asicindexcase` row that closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a load, which leaves the host ALIVE and the ISR CLOBBERED, worse than the defect being fixed; and **`kill 30 txt`**, a diagnosis sibling for the 0x10030 write arm so M_latch_silent_30 cannot delete its announce silently. *** THE TICK ROW REQUIRED A NEW OPCODE, AND THAT IS THE TRAP, NOT A DETAIL: the probe had ONLY `sw`, and a 4-byte store CANNOT SET imask0 BIT 50 because `case 0x10000` reads through `memory_readmax64`.  A tick row written with the existing helper would arm NOTHING, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***  Added `OP_SD_T0_A3` (0xFCE80000) and `OP_LD_V0_AT` (0xDC220000), and **DECODED BOTH FROM OUTSIDE THE CODE before trusting them** -- opcode 63 = sd with base $7/rt $8, opcode 55 = ld with base $1/rt $2 -- because this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap is that a wrong constant is invisible when the probe reads the same constant. **`isr keep` READS `died` PRE-FIX AND THAT IS CORRECT**: its seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts on the committed build.  That is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure, and the row comment says so. **NOT YET RUN** -- five grok seats are loading the machine and this probe's settle/spin are LOAD-SENSITIVE oracles.  The new rows are CONSTRUCTED, not MEASURED, and the pre-fix run owes a re-run with this instrument (a comparison owes ONE instrument).  *** L4 -- SIX PASS-1 SEAT CELLS CARRIED ONE STAMPED NOTE, CORRECTED 2026-09-17 FROM EACH SEAT'S OWN FILE. ***  `precommit_check.sh` section O caught it (`BOILERPLATE_FAIL`, `ip30abortkill x6`), and the defect is not untidiness.  **The six seats named FIVE different weakest dispositions** -- agy #6/#4, grok its own reframing, glm **#1**, kimi **#4**, deepseek **#5**, minimax **#6** -- **and split THREE WAYS on Q3**: agy/grok/kimi say the `0x10020` read returns **0**, deepseek says it returns **`d->isr`**, and minimax says it is **not settleable from the packet without the HEART spec**.  *A stamped note renders that as unanimity* -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape reached by a different road, since every seat here really did answer. **AND THE ERASED DISSENT WAS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE.**  minimax's refusal to guess on Q3 is what made fetching an IP30/HEART reference a decision for the owner instead of letting three seats' confident `0` stand as consensus; and grok's Q6 -- *\"that is not coverage debt, that is the defect left live\"* -- contradicts three seats who said the tick row may be filed. *The `ev` fields were per-seat and correct throughout, so the answers were never lost; only the notes were stamped.  That is why the repair was a read rather than a recovery -- and it is the argument for section O existing at all, because nothing else in the harness compares one seat's cell against another's.*  *** THE 15-ROW INSTRUMENT'S PRE-FIX BASELINE WAS RUN 2026-09-17, AND IT CAUGHT A ROW THAT WAS RED ON A CORRECT BUILD.  L1 construction repair, in-round. ***  **FIRST, THE COUNT WAS WRONG IN MY OWN NOTE:** the probe emits **18** rows, not the 15 a `grep -c '^\\s*row('` reported -- the probe's own `IP30ABORT_RESULT` line is the authority, and the grep was another correlate.  Baseline: **`IP30ABORT_RESULT=10/18`**, sentinel `IP30ABORT_DONE`, preserved at `probe_HEAD_preaccfix_20260917.out`. **SEVEN OF THE EIGHT FAILURES ARE THE DEFECT REPRODUCING**, at both directions of all three offsets plus the tick and the ISR clobber: `kill 20 rd`, `kill 28 rd`, `kill 30 wr`, `kill 20 wr`, `kill 28 wr`, `tick 50`, `isr keep`.  **Every control is GREEN** -- `live` returns `0x11223344`, the accept rows are serviced, and *** `tick 49` STAYS ALIVE WHERE `tick 50` DIES ***, which is the discriminating pair proving it is bit 50 rather than the tick path generally. *** THE EIGHTH FAILURE WAS NOT THE DEFECT AND NOT A SECOND ONE: `acc 20000` EXPECTED A COUNTER NOT TO COUNT. ***  It stored `0x1234` and wanted it back; it read `0x161c`.  **`0x161c - 0x1234 = 0x3e8 = 1000` EXACTLY**, which is `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000` in `DEVICE_TICK` -- one tick between the store and the load.  *The DEVICE was right and the ROW was wrong.*  The access arm alone reads like a plain r/w register; only the file's own `/*  A counter  */` comment and the tick body say otherwise. **AS WRITTEN IT MANUFACTURED A PHANTOM FAILURE ON A CORRECT BUILD, so it could never signal the defect this probe exists for** -- the vacuity class inside the round's own witness, and the reason it is L1 (pre-ship construction) rather than filed. **THE REPLACEMENT ORACLE IS STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES.**  The counter is always a multiple of 1000 (it starts at 0 and only ever `+= 1000`) and `0x1234` = 4660 is NOT, so `readback - 0x1234` is an exact multiple of 1000 **iff the store landed**, and is never one if the store was lost.  The row now pins THAT THE STORE REACHED `reg_0x20000` -- which the old literal compare only did by accident on a tickless run -- while tolerating any number of ticks. *Found by doing the test-first step the round already owed: re-running the pre-fix baseline on the CURRENT instrument, because the last recorded figure (6/9) came from a retired nine-row one.  That is the same two-instrument defect found in `sgirergbroundtrip` (4/6 vs 10/10) an hour earlier, caught here BEFORE it became a record rather than after.*  *** FIXED AND MEASURED 2026-09-17 AS `#531`.  PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18 [18-ROW INSTRUMENT, SUPERSEDED BY 8/21 -> 21/21 -- see the correction at the end of this note], with the re-pin's own red measured separately at 8/18 [the order proof, which STAYS on the 18-row form by design]. ***  All four guest-reachable `abort()` calls de-fatalised; live `abort()` 4 -> 0, `return` statements 6 -> 6 (no early return, the common tail still runs). **The disposition was settled by `heart.h`, the header this file cites three times**: `set_isr`/`clear_isr` are write-side (member 4 on WRITE, member 3 on READ) and `isr` is READ-ONLY, so `0x10030`'s clear-on-write is dropped -- *the file's own \"TODO: is this correct?\" answered NO by its own source*. **Two L1 row defects the fix exposed, both repaired in-round:** `acc 20000` expected a counter not to count (`0x161c - 0x1234 = 1000` exactly, the tick stride) and was RED ON A CORRECT BUILD; `isr keep` read the FIRST of three loads instead of the last, invisible pre-fix because its seeding write aborted. **OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2 on the real diff.**  *** PASS 2 DID NOT RATIFY (2026-09-17, panel _scratchpad/panel_20260917_215210).  SEVEN scriptable seats answered; the kimi CLI was on its weekly 403 wall and is COVERED by its kimi-k3:cloud substitute, not lost.  opus5 STILL RUNNING; fable5 HELD by standing directive until every other seat has spoken.  So this stage stands at 7 of 9 and any ruling drawn from it is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  FOUR findings, each verified by me against the code rather than taken on the seat's word.  (L4) the latch comment's 'ONE BIT PER SITE' is false -- five ISR paths share one bit -- REPAIRED to state the real invariant, that latch granularity must match the marker's qualification: both messages end in an unqualified '(once)', which claims one emission per device instance, so one bit apiece is what makes the marker true.  (L1) the tick pair stopped discriminating post-fix, REPAIRED by re-aiming both rows onto the announce channel and adding 'tick 50 txt'.  (L1) 'kill 30 txt' was bound to the 'isr keep' transcript and read the 0x10020 announce -- vacuous for its own named mutant; STAGED.  (L2 FLOOR BREACH) nothing observed that d->isr &= ~idata still runs at 0x10028, a clause-(d) clean sweep four seats reached independently; STAGED as row 'isr clr', with 'isr drop 30' beside it for the re-inserted clear at 0x10030.  The three staged repairs were deliberately NOT applied while the 19-row measurement was executing -- a comparison run owes ONE instrument.  Round figures will be cited from a single 21-row instrument; the 18-row form is frozen at ip30_abort_probe.18row_20260917.frozen.py so the opus table stays attributable.  *** THESE REPAIRS OWE THEIR OWN PASS 2, per the repair-chain rule. ***  *** 21-ROW FIGURES MEASURED 2026-09-17: 8/21 pre-fix, 21/21 post-fix, on ONE instrument. The 18-row pair (11/18, 18/18) is SUPERSEDED rather than stale -- both were correct measurements of an instrument that then grew by three repairs and two floor rows, and citing a pre-fix number from one instrument beside a post-fix number from another is exactly what the one-instrument rule refuses. ***  *** THE FIGURES IN THIS NOTE ARE SUPERSEDED AND ARE CORRECTED HERE RATHER THAN REWRITTEN, so the record shows what was claimed and when.  `PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18' was a correct measurement of the 18-ROW instrument, taken before pass 2.  Pass 2 then found four defects; repairing them added `tick 50 txt', re-bound `kill 30 txt', and added the two floor rows `isr clr' and `isr drop 30', growing the instrument to 21 rows.  THE ROUND'S FIGURES ARE 8/21 PRE-FIX (PREFIX_21row_20260917.out) AND 21/21 POST-FIX (probe_FIX531_21row_20260917.out), both on that one instrument.  Note also that this note's `OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2' line is DISCHARGED -- the pass ran, did not ratify, and its findings are recorded in the review entries on this row. ***  *** THE opus5 PASS-2 SEAT LANDED AND DID NOT RATIFY: SIX CLEAN SWEEPS AT 19/19. ***  The stage is now 8 of 9; fable5 remains held by standing directive.  The fix is APPROVED UNCHANGED by that seat; the DETECTOR owes a CLASS CLOSURE under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes have now found breaches of one class (announce ATTRIBUTION), so the next repair enumerates the whole space from the function's own inputs and closes every cell in ONE diff rather than cell by cell.  THE CLASS: six announce sites, of which only a SINGLE-ACCESS session can attribute one, because all five ISR sites print one identical string behind one shared bit.  *** KILL TABLE 1: THE FLOOR IS DISCHARGED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY 21/21. *** Six mutants in a scratch clone; baseline NAMED (the round's post-fix working set, not HEAD), cmp'd SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in the clone with [binary -nt source] as its own freshness proof; clone-local sentinel; restore from the named baseline, never a script backup; restore verified cmp SAME against SEC and the sentinel cleared only after the rebuild was fresh.  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE.  base 21/21 | M1_del28store 20/21 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050) | M2_reins30clear 20/21 (isr drop 30, 0x484a->0x5a5a) | M3_tickquiet 20/21 (tick 50 txt) | M4_quiet30 20/21 (kill 30 txt) | M5_bit49 19/21 (tick 50 txt AND tick 49, in OPPOSITE directions) | M6_sharedlatch 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP, ESCAPED.  EVERY REPAIR MADE IN RESPONSE TO A SEAT IS NOW MEASURED FAILABLE: M1 is the opus5 seat's severest escape (19/19 pre-repair) and the row four packet-fed seats converged on kills it; M4 is agy's cell, 19/19 on the old binding and dead on the re-bound row; M2's 0x484a matches the arithmetic derived BEFORE the run.  M5's signature is not a red but a MOVE -- the announce leaves bit 50 and appears at bit 49, which only that mutant produces.  *** M6 ESCAPED AND WAS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFORE THE RUN: PRETABLE.out (mtime 22:58:19, against the table's 22:59:36) names it, derives it -- every session is a fresh process, so no row exercises both announce classes in one device lifetime -- and discloses it.  The pre-table block asked its first mandated application to CHECK the CHANNEL rule rather than obey it; this is the first test of 'a row's channel under its mutant is knowable at WRITING time' in this project, and it HELD. ***  CLASS CLOSURE APPLIED (21 -> 27 rows) under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes found breaches of ONE class, so the whole announce-attribution space is enumerated from the function's own inputs and closed in ONE diff: the two unattributed WRITE announces, the latch COLLISION, the latch DEFEAT, and the declined read's VALUE.  Two oracle shapes are new to this probe: `latch 1x' COUNTS announces (a defeated latch reads `yes' to a presence oracle either way) and `xbit' puts BOTH announce classes in ONE device lifetime, which no fresh-process row can do.  KILL TABLE 2 IS RUNNING to VERIFY THE ENUMERATION, with PRETABLE2.out written first (23:44:37 against 23:44:43).  *** KILL TABLE 2: THE CLASS CLOSURE VERIFIED, ZERO NEW BREACHES -- the #499 stop condition MET, with its denominator stated. ***  base 27/27 | M6_sharedlatch 26/27 (xbit isr, 0->1) | M7_silent20wr 25/27 (kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr) | M8_silent28wr 26/27 (kill 28 wr txt) | M9_nolatch 26/27 (latch 1x, 2->1) | M10_mirror20 26/27 (isr 20 rd val, 0x5a5a->0x0) | M1_del28store 26/27 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, binary fresh, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  *** M6 IS THE POINT: 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP BEFORE, 26/27 AFTER. ***  DENOMINATOR: six announce sites, two latch failure modes, one declined-read value.  The two axes NOT swept -- width/len (#499's own axis) and position/translation (clause (f)) -- are DISCLOSED in the probe's contract, not left absent.  *** M9 PROVES THE COUNT ORACLE WAS NECESSARY RATHER THAN DECORATIVE: latch 1x got=2 want=1.  A PRESENCE oracle reads `yes' on the fix AND on the mutant and cannot see the defect at all -- the CHANNEL vacuity arriving one level along, inside the repair for it. ***  M1 WAS RE-RUN ON PURPOSE: #462 measured that adding rows perturbs other mutants' scores, and PRETABLE2.out stated plainly that `six new independent sessions cannot perturb it' was REASONED NOT MEASURED, naming M1 as its single test.  Identical to table 1 -- the claim is now measured.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE, WITH A COST: M6 reds xbit isr ALONE; M7 reds kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr, because the xbit session's ISR access is itself a 0x10020 write.  So xbit isr is NOT a pure collision detector, and only kill 20 wr txt separates the two causes -- the two rows are COUPLED, and the probe header now says so.  CONTRACT (clause (e)) WRITTEN INTO THE PROBE HEADER, where the floor requires it, together with the ATTRIBUTION RULE (a row can attribute an announce only from a SINGLE-ACCESS session) so a future row cannot repeat the kill-30-txt defect by accident.  The header's own stale claim -- `a WITNESS attempt, NOT a shipped detector' -- was corrected in place.  *** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: '5. `isr 20 rd' / `isr 28 rd' -- load does not clobber ISR; load's VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don't-mirror)'.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd val' and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the same pattern: grok's design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER'S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is 'a row list rewritten from a panel's findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block's own prescribed rows, not merely extended with the new ones.'  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in a SEAT'S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row list against that.  So the #490 rule was obeyed and the row was still lost: the diff ran against the wrong source document.  AND THE PRETABLE MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN NEUTRAL: PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as 'decline, no value to pin', so the one artifact #490 DOES mandate diffing against positively asserted that neither row was owed.  *A pre-table block that mis-classifies a cell does not merely fail to catch the omission; it CERTIFIES it.*  grok's correction is one sentence: A DECLINE HAS A VALUE, AND IT IS 0.  PROPOSED REMEDY, FILED NOT BUILT (a harness change owing two negative controls): a round whose fix design was reviewed by a seat DIFFS ITS FINAL ROW LIST AGAINST THAT SEAT'S PRESCRIBED ROWS, as #490 mandates for the PRETABLE -- and the PRETABLE should CARRY those prescriptions forward so the two documents cannot disagree silently.  Cheapest mechanical form is existence-only: if a FIXDIFF_<seat>_*.txt sits beside the killtable dir, each of its numbered prescriptions must map to a shipped row or an explicit no-row sentence.  *** KILL TABLE 3: THE SIBLING-PARITY REPAIR, MEASURED. ***  base 28/28 | M11_mirror28 27/28 (`isr 28 rd val', 0x5a5a -> 0x0).  Sentinels verified; restore cmp SAME against SEC; clone removed.  PRETABLE3.out written 00:49:41 against the table's 00:50:16.  *** PRETABLE.out AND PRETABLE2.out WERE DELIBERATELY NOT EDITED TO CARRY THE CORRECTION: their mtimes ARE the order proofs for tables 1 and 2, and editing either would reset the mtime and DESTROY the property the block exists to provide.  A correction to an order-proof artifact goes in the NEXT block. ***  The contract in the probe header now names BOTH read arms for the mirroring claim; it previously named only 0x10020, which was an overclaim of exactly the kind clause (e) is meant to prevent.  *** THE AF2 ADJUDICATION PASS RAN ON 8 OF 9 (seven scriptable seats + the opus5 seat outstanding at the time of writing); FABLE REMAINS HELD BY STANDING DIRECTIVE AND SPEAKS LAST.  Any ruling here is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  It found ONE floor breach (`isr 28 rd val'), which is repaired and measured, and TWO wrong warrants of mine, both corrected.  The F13 split (2 REFINE / 1 UPHOLD) is RECORDED, NOT RESOLVED -- the stopping rule files it, and a disagreement must not be settled by whoever writes it up.  *** THE opus5 AF2 SEAT: EIGHT MUTANTS, ALL 27/27 -- TOTAL CLEAN SWEEPS OF THE SHIPPED INSTRUMENT -- AND SIX LIE INSIDE CELLS PRETABLE2's OWN ENUMERATION DECLARES CLOSED. ***  Scratch clone, baseline NAMED (md5 0f012a03175541c085d90331ae395abe) and cmp SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in-clone with -nt verified, clone-local sentinel, instrument FROZEN (probe.frozen.py md5 ca309b97085608e9fb4eb0ff0be08095) for the whole comparison, every mutant dry-run-diffed to exactly one line, MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND a hard fail.  Every log ends IP30ABORT_DONE with IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK.  Artifacts PRESERVED BY THE MAIN LOOP to regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opusF2_20260918/ because the seat is read-only and said so rather than writing into the repo.  THE EIGHT: MA_assign20wr (|= -> =, 0x10020-WR latch set, ONE CHARACTER) | MD_assigntick (same at TICK) | MB_nolatchset20wr | MF_nolatchset28rd | MG_nolatchset30wr | ME_nolatchsettick (all: delete the latch SET) | MH_nocounter (delet